Scryfall publishes no Simplified-Chinese printings, so `oracle_gen`'s MTGJSON
`foreignData` sweep — the source of every other `card-data.<lng>.json` — can
never produce `zhs`. This adds the one locale from the community dataset
(HeliumOctahelide/magic-cards-zhs, weekly GitHub release) instead.

Deliberately a STANDALONE script, not an `oracle-gen` arm: `card-data.json`
already carries `scryfall_oracle_id` for every face, so the join needs no engine
code and runs in seconds rather than behind a `tool`-profile rebuild.

Three properties of the dataset had to be measured and handled:

  * 2,351 of 39,959 lines are malformed — a literal double quote escaped TWICE
    (`\\"`), which is not a legal JSON escape, so `JSON.parse` rejects the whole
    line. Repairing the raw line (`\"` → `"`) makes all 39,959 parse.
  * Every multi-line record encodes its line breaks as TWO backslashes plus `n`
    (22,195 records; 0 with a real newline). The engine's Oracle parser is
    line-oriented, so this must become a real break. Matching one backslash is
    the subtle wrong answer: it consumes the pair and leaves `\` + newline.
  * 73 occurrences across 52 records still carry Forge's `CARDNAME` template
    token, which would render literally. Expanded to the record's own localized
    name. No other template token exists in the dataset.

Quality tier `text_stage` 0 (3,192 records, unlicensed/unsourced) is dropped by
default; `--all-stages` opts back in. Records are per-PRINTING while the sidecar
is per-CARD-NAME, so ties reduce by stage, then release date, then collector
number, never by file order.

Measured on the current 362-card pool: 358/358 usable keys covered (100%).
Text is CC-BY-SA-4.0 and attributed in the script's output.
